Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period wilderness drama, naturalistic humans, dogs, snow, fur, and firelight.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Golden California light falls over orchards, lawns, and a broad veranda. BUCK, a huge four-year-old dog with a thick brown-and-black coat, white chest blaze, wolfish muzzle, and powerful St. Bernard build, bounds through the grounds.

(SOUND: birds in orchard trees, children laughing, water splashing in the cement tank)

Buck carries a small boy on his back, then settles beside JUDGE MILLER'S boots before a library window.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck lived at a big house in the sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Over this great demesne Buck ruled. He was king over all creeping, crawling, flying things of Judge Miller's place.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

MANUEL leads Buck through a dark orchard to a lonely platform. A STRANGER takes a stout rope from him as coins chink between the men.

(SOUND: distant locomotive whistle, coins clinking, wind in bare branches)

The rope tightens under Buck's collar. Buck lunges, but the Stranger throws him onto the platform and chokes him unconscious.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck had learned to trust in men he knew.

INT. SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT SHED - NIGHT

Buck lies inside a cramped wooden crate, his coat dirty and his eyes bloodshot. A saloon-keeper's candle throws sickly light across the bars.

(SOUND: crate boards rattling, foghorns, a train clattering beyond the shed)

Buck springs at every opening door, hopeful for Judge Miller, then growls at the saloon-keeper.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He lay nursing his wrath and wounded pride.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The vague sense of impending calamity oppressed him.

EXT. DOG BREAKER'S YARD - DAY

A high-walled Seattle yard holds Buck's splintering crate. THE MAN IN THE RED SWEATER, stout and calm in his sagging red wool sweater, lifts a club as Buck bursts free.

(SOUND: hatchet splitting wood, Buck snarling, men shouting from atop the wall)

Buck launches himself. The club catches him in midair and drops him hard. He charges again and again; each blow breaks the charge.

(SOUND: club striking fur and bone, Buck's ragged roar, blood dripping onto dirt)

Buck finally lies stunned, blood flecking his once-glossy coat. The man kneels, offers water, then raw meat.

THE MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction.

THE MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Be a good dog and all'll go well and the goose hang high.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

EXT. NARWHAL DECK - DAY

Buck, still massive but bruised, stands beside CURLY, a friendly black Newfoundland. Snow begins to fall as PERRAULT, a small wiry French-Canadian courier, leads them from the ship.

(SOUND: ship bell, gulls, propeller slowing, snow crunching under paws)

Buck steps into snow, recoils, sniffs it, and licks it. The snow bites his tongue and vanishes.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

A raw beach camp sprawls beneath mountains. CURLY approaches a wolfish HUSKY with her tail wagging. The husky rips her face and leaps away.

(SOUND: husky teeth snapping, Curly's scream, axes striking frozen wood)

Curly rushes again. The husky knocks her down. A silent circle of dogs closes over her in a blur of fur and blood.

FRANCOIS, a broad black-faced French-Canadian dog-driver in fur cap and heavy mittens, swings an axe and scatters the pack.

(SOUND: Francois shouting, clubs striking, dogs snarling and retreating)

Buck watches SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with narrow eyes, lick his lips and seem to laugh.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Once down, that was the end of you.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
From that moment Buck hated Spitz with a bitter and deathless hatred.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - NIGHT

Under whipping snow, Francois harnesses Buck into a sled team. DAVE, a gloomy dark-coated wheeler, and SOL-LEKS, a scarred one-eyed husky, stand rigid in their traces.

(SOUND: leather harness creaking, wind cutting across the camp, whip cracking)

Buck tangles his traces. Dave nips him; Sol-leks snaps at his shoulder. Buck corrects himself and leans into the load.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

INT. SNOW DEN - NIGHT

Buck shivers beside the glowing tent, forbidden inside. He finds BILLEE buried beneath the snow, then digs his own hollow with frantic paws.

(SOUND: wind over the snowfield, paws scraping snow, muffled dog breathing)

Buck curls into the warm pocket and sleeps. At dawn he bursts upward through a fresh layer of snow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Another lesson. So that was the way they did it.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Buck leads the team through hard blue snow, his coat now roughened by frost and trail grime. Perrault walks ahead on snowshoes while Francois guides the sled.

(SOUND: runners hissing over snow, panting dogs, harness bells)

Buck eats his fish ration quickly, then steals a chunk of bacon while Perrault's back is turned. DUB receives the blame.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He did not steal for joy of it, but because of the clamor of his stomach.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the Northland, under the law of club and fang, whoso took such things into account was a fool.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

A fire burns on frozen lake ice beneath a rock wall. Buck returns to his snow nest and finds Spitz occupying it.

(SOUND: gale screaming over the lake, fire sputtering, low canine growls)

Buck and Spitz circle, teeth bared. Suddenly starving huskies flood the camp and seize the food.

(SOUND: clubs striking ribs, bread sacks tearing, a frenzy of howls)

Buck fights three attackers, then Spitz slashes Buck's throat from the side. Francois and Perrault drive the invaders away.

FRANCOIS
Mebbe it mek you mad dog, dose many bites.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The time for the great fight had not yet come.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - NIGHT

Moonlight glazes a frozen creek. A snowshoe rabbit races ahead while sixty dogs thunder after it. Buck runs at their head, his breath streaming silver.

(SOUND: pounding paws, wild wolf-cries, rabbit shrieking)

Spitz cuts across the bend and kills the rabbit. Buck crashes into him. The dogs form a silent circle around the two rivals.

(SOUND: fangs clashing, claws scraping ice, dogs breathing in the frozen silence)

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder. Buck feints for the throat, drops low, and crushes Spitz's foreleg.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The time had come. It was to the death.

Buck breaks Spitz's other foreleg. The circle closes. Buck lunges; Spitz vanishes beneath the pack.

(SOUND: a sudden savage chorus, snow churned under fighting bodies, then wind)

Buck stands bloodied and upright beneath the moon.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - MORNING

Buck, scarred and stiff, steps into the leader's position. Francois tries to harness Sol-leks ahead of him.

(SOUND: harness buckles clicking, dogs whining, river ice cracking)

Buck drives Sol-leks aside and refuses to yield, always staying beyond Francois's club.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots into lead, traces taut, and the team surges forward as one body.

(SOUND: sled runners racing, harness bells quickening, Francois laughing)

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had earned the leadership, and he would not be content with less.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL - NIGHT

A worn mail team camps among river timber. BUCK is leaner now, his mane frosted and his ribs beginning to show. DAVE collapses beside the fire, unable to rise.

(SOUND: fire popping, Dave's low whimper, distant sled bells)

The SCOTCH HALF-BREED removes Dave from the traces. Dave bites through Sol-leks's traces and stands again in his proper place.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The pride of trace and trail was his.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL - MORNING

Dave crawls toward the harness through the snow, dragging his failing body by his forelegs.

(SOUND: Dave gasping, leather straps creaking, wind in spruce branches)

The Scotch Half-Breed walks back through the trees. Buck and the team wait, listening.

(SOUND: one distant revolver shot, then harness bells starting again)

Buck lowers his head as the sled moves on.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Buck and the surviving team stagger down a muddy spring street. HAL, a young man with a cartridge belt and hunting knife, and CHARLES, middle-aged with watery eyes and an upturned mustache, inspect them.

(SOUND: wagon wheels in slush, dogs panting, saloon piano leaking through a doorway)

MERCEDES, Charles's soft, well-dressed wife and Hal's sister, fusses over an enormous heap of luggage beside an overloaded sled.

HAL
Mush on there!

The exhausted dogs strain. The frozen runners do not move. Hal raises his whip.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't. The poor dears!

A bystander demonstrates how to break the runners loose. The sled starts, overturns on a corner, and scatters gear across the street.

(SOUND: sled crashing, tin dishes clattering, citizens laughing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They did not know how to work dogs, nor how to work themselves.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Spring water runs beneath thinning river ice. Buck is skeletal now: his brown-and-black coat hangs limp, every rib visible, white chest blaze stained with dried blood. JOHN THORNTON, a sturdy outdoorsman with a short beard and weathered face, whittles an axe handle.

(SOUND: dripping thaw water, axe blade shaving birch, weak dogs groaning)

Hal lashes Buck, who lies in the traces and will not rise. Thornton watches, jaw clenched.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

Thornton throws Hal backward and stands over Buck.

(SOUND: Mercedes screaming, Hal's knife hitting snow, river ice groaning)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

Thornton cuts Buck free. Hal, Charles, and Mercedes drive the remaining dogs onto the rotten ice.

(SOUND: sled runners fading away, deep cracks spreading across the river)

The ice collapses. Sled, dogs, and people disappear into black water.

(SOUND: ice shearing, Mercedes's distant scream, rushing water)

Thornton kneels beside Buck and gently searches his bruised body. Buck licks his hand.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S RIVER CAMP - DAY

Buck rests beside Thornton's fire, restored to a powerful one-hundred-fifty-pound animal: thick brown-and-black coat, white chest blaze, broad head, and wolfish muzzle. SKEET, a small Irish setter, washes his wounds while NIG, a huge black half-bloodhound, watches warmly.

(SOUND: river flowing, birds calling, firewood snapping)

Thornton grips Buck's head between his hands and presses his forehead to Buck's.

JOHN THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck takes Thornton's hand gently between his teeth, then releases it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Thornton's poling boat bucks through roaring rapids. HANS and PETE brace a Manila rope from the bank while Buck runs alongside, watching Thornton.

(SOUND: rapids roaring, rope snapping taut, Thornton shouting over water)

The boat flips. Thornton is swept downstream and catches a rock. Buck dives into the current.

JOHN THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Buck reaches the bank, is fitted with the rope, and launches again. This time he cuts straight into the current.

(SOUND: Buck fighting the water, Hans paying rope through gloved hands, rocks grinding underwater)

Buck strikes Thornton like a battering ram. Thornton clings to Buck's shaggy neck as Hans snubs the rope around a tree.

(SOUND: rope groaning, water crashing over bodies, men dragging them onto gravel)

Buck lies limp until Skeet licks his face and Thornton's hand rises to his fur.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nothing was too great for Buck to do when Thornton commanded.

EXT. DAWSON STREET - DAY

A thousand-pound flour sled sits frozen into hard snow. Fur-clad miners ring it. Buck stands alone in harness, magnificent and fully recovered.

(SOUND: crowd murmuring, sleigh bells, sharp cold wind)

MATTHEWSON drops a sack of gold dust onto a saloon rail. Thornton kneels beside Buck, cheek against cheek.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

JOHN THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

Thornton whispers into Buck's ear.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck grips Thornton's mittened hand in his jaws, then releases it.

(SOUND: sudden silence from the crowd, Buck's eager whine)

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck twists right. The runners crack loose.

(SOUND: ice crackling beneath runners, men drawing breath)

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck twists left. The sled breaks free.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws his entire body into the traces. The sled jerks, moves, and gathers steady momentum toward the firewood marker.

(SOUND: claws gouging packed snow, sled runners grinding, crowd erupting into cheers)

Thornton falls beside Buck at the finish, shaking him by the head.

JOHN THORNTON
You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - DAY

A spruce-bough lodge stands beside piles of moose-hide gold sacks. Thornton, Hans, and Pete wash gold from a shallow placer while Buck lies beside the fire, restless.

(SOUND: water sloshing in pans, pickaxes striking gravel, wind through spruce)

Buck hears a long howl from the forest. He rises and slips between the trees.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Deep in the forest a call was sounding.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - NIGHT

Buck enters a moonlit clearing. A long lean TIMBER WOLF stands on its haunches and howls skyward.

(SOUND: lone wolf howl, night insects, leaves stirring)

Buck approaches in a half-crouch. The wolf flees; Buck follows through the trees until the wolf turns at a timber jam.

Buck lowers his head. The wolf cautiously sniffs his nose.

(SOUND: soft canine whines, creek water moving over stones)

The two run side by side through the twilight forest. At a stream Buck stops, remembering.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck remembered John Thornton.

Buck turns back toward camp as the wolf's mournful howl fades behind him.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - DUSK

Buck returns from the forest carrying the strength and grace of a giant wolf. He stops at a fresh trail leading toward camp. The woods are unnaturally silent.

(SOUND: a single frightened squirrel chattering, distant voices chanting, Buck's low growl)

Buck finds NIG dead in brush, an arrow through his body. Farther on, HANS lies face-down, feathered with arrows.

Buck creeps to the ruined lodge. YEEHAT HUNTERS circle the wreckage, chanting. Their chief turns as Buck explodes from the trees.

(SOUND: Buck's thunderous roar, arrows whistling, Yeehat cries)

Buck tears down the YEEHAT CHIEF, then races through the hunters, ripping throats and driving them into the forest.

(SOUND: panicked feet fleeing through brush, arrows striking trees, Buck snarling)

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - NIGHT

Moonlight shines on the shattered camp. Buck finds PETE dead in his blankets. By a muddy pool, SKEET lies with her head and forepaws in the water.

(SOUND: water lapping at the bank, Buck's slow breathing, a distant owl)

Buck follows Thornton's scent into the pool. Nothing comes out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken.

A wolf pack enters the clearing in a silver flood. Buck stands still before them.

(SOUND: many wolves yelping, paws moving through grass, a rising wind)

The boldest wolf leaps. Buck breaks its neck. Three more attack; Buck sends them back bleeding.

The long lean timber wolf approaches, whining softly. Buck recognizes his wild brother. They touch noses.

(SOUND: soft whines, then an old wolf's long rising howl)

An old scarred wolf howls at the moon. The pack joins him. Buck raises his head and answers.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And Buck ran with them, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he ran.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - SUMMER DAY

Long grass grows over the old gold sacks. A great wolf with Buck's brown muzzle, white chest blaze, and massive frame stands alone beside the hidden mine.

(SOUND: summer wind through grass, a single long mournful howl)

Buck howls once toward the empty valley, then turns and disappears into the timber.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He may be seen running at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His great throat sings the song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT

THE END
